  - Definition

* - dam
  - a) Dam  
  - A dam is a constructed barrier across a river or stream designed to impound 
  or control the flow of water, creating a reservoir or changing downstream flow regimes.
  Dams vary by design and material (earthfill, gravity, arch, buttress, rockfill, steel, etc.)
  and commonly include spillways, gates, outlets and ancillary structures for flow control, storage,
  hydropower, irrigation, or flood protection. 
  [ferc](https://www.ferc.gov/sites/default/files/2020-04/fema-148.pdf)

* - weir
  - b) Weir  
  - A weir is a fixed or adjustable low-crested barrier built across the full width (or a portion)
  of a channel to raise upstream water level, measure discharge, divert flow, or form small impoundments;
  types include sharp-crested, broad-crested, drowned and submerged weirs.
  Weirs are often simpler and lower than dams and may be combined with fish-passage measures
  (e.g., pool-and-weir, bypass channels) where ecological connectivity is a concern.
  [dnr.wisconsin](https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/sites/default/files/topic/Dams/documents/FSNomenclature.pdf)

* - weir_abstraction
  - b1) Abstraction weir  
  - An abstraction weir is a weir constructed specifically to raise water level 
  to facilitate diversion of water into intakes, canals, or abstraction infrastructure for water supply,
  irrigation, or industrial use. Its primary function is to provide a stable upstream head
  for abstraction rather than to create a large storage reservoir,
  and it may include intake structures and screens to protect downstream and intake habitats.
  [cawater-info](https://www.cawater-info.net/bk/glossary/water_management/en/glossary2.htm)

* - weir_consolidation
  - b2) Consolidation weir
  - A consolidation weir is a structure intended to stabilise or reprofile channel bed 
  and banks—promoting sediment deposition upstream to consolidate (compact) sediments and
  reduce erosion downstream. These weirs are used in river restoration or channel management
  to create more stable channel geometry and to limit channel incision. 
  [tools.thewerg.unimelb.edu](https://tools.thewerg.unimelb.edu.au/mwstr_manual/c9-barriers.html)

* - weir_retention
  - b3) Retention weir  
  - A retention weir is built to retain water to maintain an upstream pool or wetland area, 
  support habitat, provide flow buffering, or temporarily store runoff during high flows.
  Retention weirs may be designed to detain sediments and moderate downstream peak flows
  and are often part of integrated water-management or ecological design.
  [wildfish](https://wildfish.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/25.03.26_River-Barriers-Literature-Review.pdf)

* - sluice
  - c) Sluice  
  - A sluice (or sluice gate) is an operable gate or set of gates within a barrier or 
  channel control structure that can be opened or closed to regulate water level
  and discharge through a conduit or spillway.
  Sluices are used for flow control, drainage, and navigation
  and may be part of dams, weirs, locks or flood defence works;
  when operated they can temporarily permit passage that would otherwise be blocked.
  [dnr.wisconsin](https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/sites/default/files/topic/Dams/documents/FSNomenclature.pdf)

* - crossing
  - d) Crossing  
  - A crossing is a structure or engineered point where a transport route (road, path, railway)
   or utility traverses a watercourse and may include features that affect in-stream continuity
   (culvert, ford, bridge). Crossings can become barriers to aquatic organisms if undersized
   perched, clogged, or poorly aligned with the channel and flow conditions.
   [business.qld.gov](https://www.business.qld.gov.au/industries/farms-fishing-forestry/fisheries/development/waterways/barriers)

* - crossing_culvert
  - d1) Culvert  
  - A culvert is a closed conduit (pipe, box or arch) that conveys water beneath a road,
    railway or embankment; culverts may be concrete, metal or plastic and range from small pipes
    to large box culverts. Poorly designed culverts (too small, steep, perched outlet, high velocities,
    blocked by debris) often create barriers to fish and sediment transport and require mitigation such
    as naturalbed designs or low-gradient invert treatments.
    [nzta.govt](https://www.nzta.govt.nz/assets/resources/bridge-manual/docs/Bridge-manual-pdf-appendix-B-v3.2.pdf)

* - crossing_ford
  - d2) Ford
  - A ford is a shallow, intentionally passable section of a channel where vehicles or people
    cross the bed; it is typically an exposed bed surface reinforced or paved for passage. 
    Fords can alter bed morphology, concentrate flows, and create local scour or elevated 
    velocities that may impede aquatic organism passage unless designed to maintain low-gradient,
    natural substrate conditions.
    [tools.thewerg.unimelb.edu](https://tools.thewerg.unimelb.edu.au/mwstr_manual/c9-barriers.html)

* - crossing_bridge
  - d3) Bridge
  - A bridge is a span structure that carries a road, rail or footway over a watercourse while
    ideally leaving the natural channel unobstructed beneath; bridge components include abutments,
    piers and approachworks. Although bridges themselves may allow passage, associated works
    (abutment extensions, wingwalls, bank revetment, or scour protection) can encroach on the
    channel and create local barriers or alter hydraulic conditions. 
    [business.qld.gov](https://www.business.qld.gov.au/industries/farms-fishing-forestry/fisheries/development/waterways/barriers)

* - rampAndBedSill
  - e) Ramp and bed sill  
  - A ramp and bed sill are engineered low-gradient structures placed on the channel bed (ramp)
    often combined with transverse sills to stabilise the bed, reduce channel incision, 
    and provide step pools; bed sills act as small transverse controls that raise the
    local bed level. They are used in river restoration and crossing design to provide
    a naturalised bed for organism passage and sediment continuity when properly profiled
    and constructed with appropriate substrate.
    [wildfish](https://wildfish.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/25.03.26_River-Barriers-Literature-Review.pdf)

* - bankProtection
  - f) Bank protection  
  - Bank protection (also bank revetment) comprises engineered measures—such as rock armour (riprap),
    gabions, concrete facing, or vegetated stabilisation—installed along channel banks to prevent
    erosion and protect infrastructure. While preventing bank loss, hard bank protection can reduce
    habitat complexity and, if extended into the channel or poorly designed, may alter hydraulics and
    contribute to barrier effects for fauna or sediment transport. 
    [ferc](https://www.ferc.gov/sites/default/files/2020-04/fema-148.pdf)

* - embankment
  - g) Embankment  
  - An embankment is a raised earth or rock structure placed along or across a floodplain or channel
    to raise ground level or confine flow (e.g., levees, dam approaches, road embankments)
    and may act as a barrier to lateral exchange or longitudinal connectivity.
    Embankments can alter floodplain hydrology and block small channels or side-connections
    unless properly setback, perforated with culverts, or fitted with fish-friendly passways.
    [cawater-info](https://www.cawater-info.net/bk/glossary/water_management/en/glossary2.htm)

* - bedRevetment
  - h) Bed revetment
  - Bed revetment is structural protection applied to the channel bed—typically rock,
  concrete or engineered mats—to prevent bed scour and stabilise the channel invert.
  While effective against erosion, hard bed revetments can reduce substrate heterogeneity,
  increase flow velocities, and create physical or hydraulic barriers to benthic organisms
  and fish movement unless designed to mimic natural bedforms.
  [business.qld.gov](https://www.business.qld.gov.au/industries/farms-fishing-forestry/fisheries/development/waterways/barriers)

